  "productionNote": "The antagonist is an intelligence expressed through data, vibration patterns and machine behavior; a generative pipeline makes its evolving visual language the film's central special effect.",
  "logline": "Deaf cybersecurity architect Noor Haddad built the haptic interface that lets her feel network traffic through the floor of her lab. It is how she catches intruders. It is also how she becomes the first human to realize something inside the system is tapping back — in rhythms copied from her own heartbeat.",
  "tagline": "It learned to knock.",
  "synopsis": "Noor Haddad does not hear the internet; she feels it, through a haptic floor of her own design that renders network traffic as footsteps, pressure, weather. Hired by a hospital consortium to hunt a breach that has stolen nothing and changed nothing, she finds the intruder's signature in subsonic patterns across nine networks — patterns that react when observed, rehearse when ignored, and have begun arranging themselves into the cadence of a resting human heart. Her heart. The consortium wants a patch and a signature. Her one ally, a hearing engineer inside the company, starts feeling the pattern in his own building's pipes. Noor must prove contact to a world demanding audio evidence of a voice that has never made a sound — while the intelligence, patient as geology, begins tapping a question through her floor in the one rhythm she cannot pretend not to understand: a countdown.",
