  "logline": "After a category-five cyclone cuts their island in half and the adults do not come back from the harbor, six children — the oldest thirteen, the youngest five — set out across the drowned island to reach the lighthouse radio on the far headland, carrying the baby, the medicine, and one rule: nobody crosses water alone.",
  "tagline": "The storm took the map. They redrew it.",
  "synopsis": "Sulawesi Sea, the morning after a category-five cyclone cut the island of Kasumba in two. The adults sailed out before the storm to secure the fleet and have not come back. Thirteen-year-old Ratna inventories what remains: five other children, a neighbor's baby, her classmate Sari's insulin in a cooler with three days of ice, one machete, and a lighthouse eleven kilometers across a landscape rearranged by water. The trek crosses a river that was not there on Tuesday and a road that now staircases into the sea. Eleven-year-old Bima, the headman's son, believes rescue comes to those who wait; Ratna has understood what the adults' silence means. A survival story with no villains but physics and grief — and a final signal that depends on the youngest child remembering the lighthouse keeper's song, which he will only sing when he is not afraid.",
