  "logline": "On the one-way Sunward mission to seed a habitat around a dying star's last light, astronaut Elias Vane begins receiving transmissions from a woman named Sol who tends the station he will build — forty years after he will finish it, and eleven years after he will die.",
  "tagline": "She is waiting at the end of his life.",
  "synopsis": "The Sunward mission is honest about its terms: Elias Vane, sixty-one, widowed, the finest deep-space engineer alive, will fly twenty-two years to a dying star's cinder, build the lighthouse station future crews will navigate by, and not come home. In year three the capsule begins receiving narrow-band letters in an obsolete encoding — from Sol Park, keeper of the station he will build, writing forty years after he finishes it and eleven years after he dies. She describes storms he will design the shields against, jokes he has not made yet, a garden he will plant in a bay he has not welded. Mission control calls it fraud, then physics, then nothing. Elias answers, knowing every reply takes longer than the last, and that Sol is bound by a promise she says he made her: never to tell him the one thing he asks.",
