  "synopsis": "For nine hundred years the Firekeepers have tended the Perpetual Hearth, the flame from which every city relights its forges and winter stoves. Now the sun is failing — crops grey at noon, seas skin with ice in summer — and the order reveals its oldest secret: the flame is not a symbol of the sun. It is a seed of it, plantable in the sky at the World's Edge, if a keeper can carry it there alive. Every kingdom wants the planting under its own flag, or not at all. Ash, seventeen, an apprentice who tends the scullery fires, takes the flame the night the Hearth is betrayed from inside — or steals it; the masters' contradictory orders will let history decide. Her crossing becomes an education in the arithmetic of warmth: every village she lights from the lantern spends the world's savings, and the dark keeps the accounts.",
